<!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:heading {"level":3,"className":"hf-h3 sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading hf-h3 sp-reveal sp-reveal--up">A licence starts on the ground, with the wind.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"} -->
<p class="sp-reveal sp-reveal--up">Learning to fly is not about memorizing complex avionics from day one. It begins by standing on the grass at Brackmoor, feeling the direction and strength of the breeze, and watching the windsock swing across runway 09/27. When you understand how the air moves across the airfield, the aircraft becomes an intuitive tool rather than an intimidating machine.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"} -->
<p class="sp-reveal sp-reveal--up">Since 2011, Margit Halvorsen and Ruth Abernathy have kept training personal and grounded. Every flight pairs you directly with an instructor who knows these local skies and pastures. You learn by doing: checking fuel samples in the morning light, feeling the controls answer your touch, and making steady, confident decisions from pre-flight walk-round to final touchdown.</p>
